+ * The Mono calling convention of the bundled LLVM 16 (LLVMMono1CallConv, CC 21) lowers arguments as base AAPCS on ARM: floats and
+ * doubles go in core registers / on the stack. Mono's armhf ABI (mini-arm.c get_call_info: add_float) -- its own backend, the
+ * interpreter's entry trampolines (CallContext), gsharedvt, every other caller or callee -- passes them in s0-s15 / d0-d7 with
+ * back-filling, which is what LLVM's C convention does with -float-abi=hard (AAPCS-VFP). Returns agree in both conventions, and so
+ * do signatures without float arguments (core registers + stack, the same rules).
+ *
+ * CC 21 also differs from the C convention in r8 (MONO_ARCH_RGCTX_REG = MONO_ARCH_IMT_REG = ARMREG_V5): an "inreg" argument goes
+ * there, and a CC 21 CALL does not preserve r8 (static rgctx / ftnptr-arg / IMT trampolines load it on the way to the callee), while
+ * a C-convention call does. The C convention's swifterror argument is exactly r8 in and r8 clobbered (ARMCallingConv.td:
+ * CCIfSwiftError -> R8; CSR_AAPCS_SwiftError). So a managed signature that carries a float / double argument gets:
+ *   - the C convention, at its definition and at every call site;
+ *   - one swifterror argument: the rgctx / IMT argument when the method or call has one (where CC 21 has "inreg"), else a trailing
+ *     one (calls pass the function's swifterror alloca without a value; definitions ignore it). The definition loads its rgctx from it
+ *     at entry and stores undef into it (r8 is not preserved, as for CC 21's callers);
+ *   - refused (Mono's own backend compiles the method) when an argument is an HFA (pinvoke signatures only) or a float argument
+ *     would go past s15 (on the stack, or Mono's s16 for a double after 15 singles), where Mono's add_float and AAPCS-VFP part; or
+ *     when it is a pinvoke-flagged definition (a native-to-managed wrapper, called from C: plain C convention) with an rgctx.
+ *   Every other signature keeps CC 21, so its code is what it was.
